WEBVTT

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.522
Geordi?

00:00:14.564 --> 00:00:16.623
In here, Data.

00:00:18.702 --> 00:00:19.896
I've got her now.

00:00:20.003 --> 00:00:21.095
She's trapped.

00:00:21.138 --> 00:00:23.504
Come on out of there,
you ungra...

00:00:24.574 --> 00:00:26.701
Hey, hey, don't you spit at me.

00:00:26.743 --> 00:00:29.041
Why is Spot under the bed?

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:32.310
Probably because she knows
if I catch her

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:34.112
I'm going to kill her.

00:00:34.151 --> 00:00:36.278
Has Spot been misbehaving?

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.188
So far, she's broken a vase,
a teapot

00:00:40.223 --> 00:00:41.884
she's ruined one of my chairs

00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:43.415
using it as a scratch post

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.518
and she's coughing

00:00:44.561 --> 00:00:46.529
hair balls up
all over my carpet.

00:00:46.563 --> 00:00:49.088
These incidents are common
to cat owners.

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:52.693
When you borrowed Spot, you said
you wanted to experience

00:00:52.736 --> 00:00:55.102
the full range
of feline behavior

00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:56.730
before getting a cat yourself.

00:00:56.773 --> 00:00:58.502
Yeah, well, I'm not ready.

00:00:58.542 --> 00:01:01.102
You can take her back
with my blessings.

00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:03.874
Go ahead, call her.

00:01:03.914 --> 00:01:06.178
Spot does not respond
to verbal commands.

00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:08.150
She doesn't come
when you call her?

00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:11.243
Data...

00:01:11.288 --> 00:01:14.280
have you ever considered
training this cat?

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.724
I never found it
to be necessary.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.160
Necessary? Data,
the cat is out of control.

00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:22.165
Half the time, I didn't know
if she was going lick me

00:01:22.199 --> 00:01:23.530
or scratch my face off.

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:26.764
I have never experienced
this kind of behavior in Spot.

00:01:26.803 --> 00:01:29.328
Although she does have
the unfortunate habit

00:01:29.373 --> 00:01:32.467
of jumping on my computer
console when I am working.

00:01:33.844 --> 00:01:35.141
She needs training.

00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:36.611
Riker to senior staff.

00:01:36.646 --> 00:01:39.274
Please report
to the observation lounge.

00:01:39.316 --> 00:01:41.113
Mission briefing in ten minutes.

00:01:41.151 --> 00:01:43.517
All right, we have to get
her out of here.

00:01:43.553 --> 00:01:46.249
I'll scare her, you grab her
when she comes out, okay?

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:48.416
I do not think it would be
wise to startle her.

00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:59.625
Training... definitely.

00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:36.070
Space, the final frontier.

00:02:36.105 --> 00:02:39.905
These are the voyages
of the Starship Enterprise.

00:02:39.976 --> 00:02:41.910
Its continuing mission...

00:02:41.978 --> 00:02:45.914
to explore strange new worlds

00:02:45.948 --> 00:02:50.385
to seek out new life
and new civilizations

00:02:50.419 --> 00:02:54.913
to boldly go
where no one has gone before.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.749
Captain's Log, Stardate 47310.2.

00:03:56.785 --> 00:03:58.309
We are investigating

00:03:58.353 --> 00:04:01.117
the disappearance
of the Medical Transport Fleming

00:04:01.156 --> 00:04:03.124
somewhere
in the Hekaras Corridor.

00:04:03.158 --> 00:04:06.389
Our search is complicated
by the unique properties

00:04:06.428 --> 00:04:08.726
of this particular
region of space.

00:04:08.763 --> 00:04:11.391
The Fleming last contacted
Starfleet four days ago.

00:04:11.433 --> 00:04:13.765
According to them, there was
nothing out of the ordinary

00:04:13.802 --> 00:04:15.133
when they entered the Corridor.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:16.262
Data.

00:04:16.304 --> 00:04:19.273
The unusually intense
tetryon field in this sector

00:04:19.307 --> 00:04:21.332
poses a severe
navigational hazard

00:04:21.376 --> 00:04:22.866
to warp-driven vessels.

00:04:22.944 --> 00:04:24.343
The Hekaras Corridor

00:04:24.379 --> 00:04:27.871
is the only route in this sector
which is free of tetryon fields.

00:04:27.983 --> 00:04:30.451
Ships traveling at warp
must use the Corridor

00:04:30.485 --> 00:04:31.713
to ensure safe passage

00:04:31.753 --> 00:04:32.879
through the region.

00:04:32.921 --> 00:04:35.549
How long will it take
to complete a level-1 search?

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:36.818
At least two days, sir.

00:04:36.858 --> 00:04:39.326
The Corridor is over
12 light-years long

00:04:39.361 --> 00:04:41.886
and the surrounding
tetryon interference

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.488
will limit our sensor range.

00:04:43.532 --> 00:04:45.193
Could we send out
reconnaissance probes

00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:46.393
to supplement the sensors?

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:48.163
Won't be very effective.

00:04:48.203 --> 00:04:49.693
The interference
will make it difficult

00:04:49.738 --> 00:04:51.137
to maintain contact
with the probes.

00:04:51.173 --> 00:04:53.698
Best we could do is
to increase sensor efficiency.

00:04:53.742 --> 00:04:55.369
We've installed
multiphase buffers

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.139
on all the sensor modules,
Captain.

00:04:57.179 --> 00:04:58.908
It should help a little.

00:04:58.947 --> 00:05:00.380
Hekaras II is inhabited,
isn't it?

00:05:00.415 --> 00:05:02.280
Maybe they've had
contact with the Fleming.

00:05:02.317 --> 00:05:03.409
They haven't.

00:05:03.451 --> 00:05:05.681
I've already spoken
to the Hekaran government.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.312
According to them, only one ship

00:05:07.355 --> 00:05:09.687
has passed through the system
in the last week.

00:05:09.724 --> 00:05:11.191
It was a Ferengi trader.

00:05:11.226 --> 00:05:13.786
The Fleming was carrying
a supply of rare biomimetic gel

00:05:13.828 --> 00:05:14.852
which is very valuable.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.455
Is it possible that the Ferengi
might have hijacked it?

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:19.330
I wouldn't put it past them.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:21.858
I think we should prepare
for that contingency.

00:05:21.903 --> 00:05:23.165
I agree.

00:05:23.205 --> 00:05:24.172
All right then.

00:05:24.206 --> 00:05:26.800
Mr. Worf, initiate
your search pattern.

00:05:26.841 --> 00:05:28.172
Let's head in.

00:05:34.082 --> 00:05:36.209
Geordi?

00:05:36.251 --> 00:05:38.742
There has been a slight drop
in sensor efficiency.

00:05:38.787 --> 00:05:41.119
Perhaps we should examine
the phase buffers.

00:05:41.156 --> 00:05:43.351
No, problem, Data.
I'll be right with you.

00:05:43.391 --> 00:05:45.859
Okay, Hansen, transfer
the EPS conduit 13

00:05:45.894 --> 00:05:47.555
to the stabilizer matrix.

00:05:47.596 --> 00:05:49.154
Aye, sir.

00:05:50.665 --> 00:05:52.895
Is there a problem
with the engines?

00:05:53.001 --> 00:05:54.298
No.

00:05:54.336 --> 00:05:56.827
Then why are you stabilizing
the EPS conduit?

00:05:56.871 --> 00:06:00.170
Just trying to get a slightly
higher power conversion level.

00:06:01.543 --> 00:06:04.410
But that would not affect
the engines in any way.

00:06:04.446 --> 00:06:05.504
I know that, Data.

00:06:05.547 --> 00:06:06.673
It's not the point.

00:06:06.715 --> 00:06:07.841
What is the point?

00:06:07.882 --> 00:06:09.315
I'm just trying to get

00:06:09.351 --> 00:06:11.819
a higher conversion level,
that's all.

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:16.550
Why?

00:06:18.994 --> 00:06:20.689
You know the Intrepid.

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:22.026
Yes.

00:06:22.063 --> 00:06:24.258
Well, their Chief Engineer
is Commander Donald Kaplan.

00:06:24.299 --> 00:06:26.062
He and I went through
the Academy together.

00:06:26.101 --> 00:06:27.534
I just like to make sure

00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:29.400
that our power conversion levels

00:06:29.437 --> 00:06:31.564
are a little bit higher
than theirs.

00:06:31.606 --> 00:06:32.868
I understand.

00:06:32.974 --> 00:06:35.408
You are in competition
with Mr. Kaplan.

00:06:35.443 --> 00:06:36.808
You might say that.

00:06:36.845 --> 00:06:38.369
This is the flagship.

00:06:38.413 --> 00:06:40.244
We should be better
than everybody else.

00:06:40.282 --> 00:06:44.082
Then you are trying
to outperform the Intrepid?

00:06:44.119 --> 00:06:46.349
Actually, it's more
a matter of personal pride.

00:06:46.388 --> 00:06:47.753
I mean, these are my engines.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:52.223
There's nothing wrong
with a little friendly rivalry.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:53.750
Okay. Computer

00:06:53.795 --> 00:06:57.231
how much have the conversion
levels increased?

00:06:57.265 --> 00:06:59.563
Levels are unchanged.

00:07:03.038 --> 00:07:05.563
All right, I'll deal
with this later.

00:07:05.607 --> 00:07:08.542
Let me give you a hand
with those sensors.

00:07:16.885 --> 00:07:18.147
How's that?

00:07:18.186 --> 00:07:20.381
One moment.

00:07:20.422 --> 00:07:24.552
The phase buffer is operating
within normal parameters.

00:07:24.592 --> 00:07:29.188
Sensor efficiency
has increased by... 6.7%.

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:30.595
Six point seven?

00:07:30.632 --> 00:07:34.659
One of the stages must
still be out of alignment.

00:07:34.703 --> 00:07:37.331
Let's try junction A-9.

00:07:40.875 --> 00:07:44.606
Geordi, I have taken
your suggestion regarding Spot.

00:07:44.646 --> 00:07:47.012
Coming down.

00:07:47.048 --> 00:07:49.016
I have begun training her.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.211
Really?

00:07:51.252 --> 00:07:54.517
Yes, I am studying
several new techniques.

00:07:54.556 --> 00:07:55.989
Huh.

00:07:57.359 --> 00:08:00.522
I began with simple
conditioned response exercises

00:08:00.562 --> 00:08:02.962
and followed
with environmental enhancement.

00:08:02.997 --> 00:08:06.330
Next, I plan to explore
bioconditioning devices.

00:08:06.368 --> 00:08:07.596
Devices?!

00:08:07.635 --> 00:08:10.536
Such as sensor nets
for behavior modification

00:08:10.572 --> 00:08:12.199
or biofeedback motivators.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.368
Unfortunately, I have been
less than successful.

00:08:18.613 --> 00:08:20.376
I've got an idea.

00:08:20.415 --> 00:08:21.814
How about a phaser?

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:24.045
A low stun setting
at just the right moment

00:08:24.085 --> 00:08:26.280
might do the trick.

00:08:26.321 --> 00:08:29.654
Geordi, I cannot stun my cat.

00:08:32.026 --> 00:08:33.618
I was kidding, Data.

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:38.256
Let's see what we've got.

00:08:43.271 --> 00:08:45.205
No, this isn't it, either.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.141
Clearly, there is a misalignment
somewhere in this system.

00:08:48.176 --> 00:08:49.734
That's for sure.

00:08:49.778 --> 00:08:53.009
Well, we've got
one more possibility.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.579
Junction at C-12?

00:09:01.656 --> 00:09:04.750
I am somewhat concerned
that my training efforts

00:09:04.793 --> 00:09:06.158
will ultimately fail.

00:09:06.194 --> 00:09:09.595
Maybe you're just going about it
the wrong way, Data.

00:09:09.631 --> 00:09:12.828
I have consulted numerous
animal training manuals.

00:09:12.901 --> 00:09:16.496
Some of them claim that cats
are inherently untrainable.

00:09:16.538 --> 00:09:18.768
Ah, I don't believe
that for a second.

00:09:18.807 --> 00:09:19.796
Listen to me...

00:09:19.841 --> 00:09:22.173
my sister didn't know
a thing about animals

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:24.110
and she was able
to train her cat.

00:09:24.145 --> 00:09:25.703
How complicated could it be?

00:09:26.714 --> 00:09:28.841
What did she train
her cat to do?

00:09:28.883 --> 00:09:31.477
She had that cat jumping
into her arm on command.

00:09:31.519 --> 00:09:32.577
Interesting.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.850
Perhaps I could modify
your sister's techniques

00:09:34.956 --> 00:09:36.856
to keep Spot from
jumping on the console.

00:09:36.958 --> 00:09:39.188
Do you know how she was able
to train her cat?

00:09:39.227 --> 00:09:42.128
Well, as I recall, she walked
around for two months

00:09:42.163 --> 00:09:44.393
with a piece of tuna
in her blouse.

00:09:44.432 --> 00:09:45.660
Hmm.

00:09:56.144 --> 00:09:57.873
I was right.

00:09:57.946 --> 00:10:00.107
This buffer is out of alignment.

00:10:03.051 --> 00:10:05.611
Okay, try that.

00:10:05.653 --> 00:10:08.417
Phase alignment is stabilized.

00:10:08.456 --> 00:10:11.186
However, sensor efficiency

00:10:11.226 --> 00:10:15.458
has increased by only
an additional 1.3%.

00:10:15.497 --> 00:10:16.464
Well, it's not much

00:10:16.498 --> 00:10:18.125
but every little
bit's going to help.

00:10:18.166 --> 00:10:19.292
Riker to Data.

00:10:19.334 --> 00:10:20.562
Data here.

00:10:20.602 --> 00:10:23.196
We're picking up a ship ahead
on long-range sensors.

00:10:23.238 --> 00:10:25.035
If you and Geordi
are through down there

00:10:25.073 --> 00:10:26.734
we'd like to have you
on the Bridge.

00:10:26.774 --> 00:10:27.741
We're not going to squeeze

00:10:27.775 --> 00:10:29.174
anything else
out of these systems.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.610
Acknowledged, sir.
We are on our way.

00:10:33.214 --> 00:10:35.239
We are within visual range, sir.

00:10:35.283 --> 00:10:36.341
On screen.

00:10:37.752 --> 00:10:40.016
It is a Ferengi transport ship.

00:10:40.054 --> 00:10:41.715
D'Kora-class.

00:10:41.756 --> 00:10:44.020
That's the same Ferengi vessel

00:10:44.058 --> 00:10:45.821
that entered the Corridor
a week ago.

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:49.023
Captain, there are no emissions
from their plasma vents.

00:10:49.063 --> 00:10:51.224
Their warp drive
must be completely inactive.

00:10:51.266 --> 00:10:52.255
That is correct, sir.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:54.097
Their impulse system
is down as well.

00:10:54.135 --> 00:10:55.864
Power generation
is at extremely low levels.

00:10:55.904 --> 00:10:58.702
What about life signs?

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.834
Life-support systems are also
functioning at low levels.

00:11:01.876 --> 00:11:04.003
There are approximately
450 Ferengi on board.

00:11:04.045 --> 00:11:07.344
That is a standard complement
for a ship of that class.

00:11:07.382 --> 00:11:11.614
Mr. Worf,
open a hailing frequency.

00:11:12.787 --> 00:11:14.846
There is no response
on any channel, sir.

00:11:14.889 --> 00:11:16.322
I'm not picking up

00:11:16.357 --> 00:11:18.882
any subspace emissions
from the ship at all, Captain.

00:11:18.993 --> 00:11:22.053
Looks like every one of their
field coils has been overloaded.

00:11:22.096 --> 00:11:25.065
That would explain why they
haven't responded to our hails.

00:11:25.099 --> 00:11:28.500
It would also explain
the failure of their warp drive.

00:11:28.536 --> 00:11:30.868
Captain, the Ferengi sensors
are still on line.

00:11:30.905 --> 00:11:33.100
I could modify one
of our deflector emitters

00:11:33.141 --> 00:11:34.836
to transmit
old-style delta waves.

00:11:34.876 --> 00:11:36.639
If I modulate that
with a com signal

00:11:36.678 --> 00:11:38.839
the Ferengi should be able
to pick that up.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:39.869
Make it so.

00:11:39.914 --> 00:11:41.404
If they did hijack the Fleming

00:11:41.449 --> 00:11:43.747
they sure didn't get
very far with the cargo.

00:11:43.785 --> 00:11:45.912
Mr. Worf, have tractor
beams standing by.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.488
We may have to take
them under tow.

00:11:48.523 --> 00:11:50.650
Helm, take us
within tractor range.

00:11:50.692 --> 00:11:51.716
Aye, sir.

00:11:51.759 --> 00:11:54.250
Captain I am picking up
power emissions

00:11:54.295 --> 00:11:55.819
from the Ferengi vessel.

00:11:55.863 --> 00:11:57.387
They are locking weapons.

00:11:57.432 --> 00:11:58.763
Shields up!

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:11.137
Ready phasers.
Prepare to return fire.

00:12:11.179 --> 00:12:13.113
Target their weapons array only.

00:12:13.147 --> 00:12:14.079
Phasers locked.

00:12:14.115 --> 00:12:15.275
Fire!

00:12:18.353 --> 00:12:21.186
Direct hit...
their weapon systems are down.

00:12:21.222 --> 00:12:22.246
Damage report.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:23.882
We sustained minimal damage

00:12:23.925 --> 00:12:25.688
to decks five and seven, sir.

00:12:25.727 --> 00:12:28.321
Captain, the Ferengi ship's
power has stabilized.

00:12:28.363 --> 00:12:30.661
They appear to have
impulse capability.

00:12:30.698 --> 00:12:32.222
They were just playing dead...

00:12:32.267 --> 00:12:34.758
conserving power until
we got in close enough.

00:12:34.802 --> 00:12:36.793
Then why would they attack us
in the first place?

00:12:36.838 --> 00:12:37.805
Somehow, I don't think

00:12:37.839 --> 00:12:39.830
they'll let us beam
over there and ask them.

00:12:39.874 --> 00:12:42.502
Mr. La Forge, how are you coming
with that com link?

00:12:42.543 --> 00:12:44.408
Almost ready, Captain.

00:12:44.445 --> 00:12:47.175
All right, let's give it a try.

00:12:47.215 --> 00:12:49.581
Open a channel.

00:13:01.162 --> 00:13:05.189
This is Captain Jean-Luc Picard
of the Enterprise.

00:13:05.233 --> 00:13:08.828
Will you please explain
why you attacked us?

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.098
Our attack?

00:13:10.138 --> 00:13:13.107
We were defending ourselves.

00:13:13.141 --> 00:13:15.473
Do you deny
that you are responsible

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.102
for disabling my vessel?

00:13:17.145 --> 00:13:21.411
DaiMon, your ship was adrift,
your communications inoperable.

00:13:21.449 --> 00:13:24.282
We were attempting
to assess your status.

00:13:24.319 --> 00:13:27.186
You were attempting to move in
for the kill.

00:13:27.221 --> 00:13:29.689
Do not toy with me, Picard.

00:13:29.724 --> 00:13:33.888
We are obviously at your mercy,
but know this:

00:13:33.961 --> 00:13:37.362
When the Ferengi Council
learns of your actions

00:13:37.398 --> 00:13:40.128
they will consider this
an act of war.

00:13:40.168 --> 00:13:43.604
DaiMon, clearly, there has been
some misunderstanding.

00:13:43.638 --> 00:13:45.731
I think it would be
in all our interests

00:13:45.773 --> 00:13:49.106
if you were to come aboard
and we discussed this situation.

00:13:49.143 --> 00:13:52.635
I see no reason to trust you,
human.

00:13:54.015 --> 00:13:57.542
In that case,
we'll be on our way.

00:13:57.585 --> 00:14:01.453
I will relay your position
to your government, if you wish

00:14:01.489 --> 00:14:04.583
in case you're unable
to complete your repairs

00:14:04.625 --> 00:14:06.320
and you remain stranded.

00:14:08.629 --> 00:14:09.960
Perhaps, uh...

00:14:09.997 --> 00:14:13.899
it would be better
to discuss the situation.

00:14:22.477 --> 00:14:26.573
We detected what appeared to be
a Federation signal buoy.

00:14:26.614 --> 00:14:28.377
When we approached it

00:14:28.416 --> 00:14:31.385
it emitted
a massive verteron pulse.

00:14:31.419 --> 00:14:33.216
Our warp drive, our sensors

00:14:33.254 --> 00:14:36.348
our communications systems
were all disabled.

00:14:36.391 --> 00:14:42.330
We assumed we were the victims
of a new Federation weapon.

00:14:42.363 --> 00:14:45.355
The Federation
established this corridor

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.392
to ensure a safe access
through this sector.

00:14:48.436 --> 00:14:50.904
We have nothing to gain
by mining it.

00:14:50.972 --> 00:14:52.496
Whatever happened to your ship

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.531
may not have been
an isolated incident.

00:14:54.575 --> 00:14:56.133
A Federation medical transport

00:14:56.177 --> 00:14:57.906
was recently lost
in that corridor.

00:14:58.012 --> 00:15:00.003
We passed a Federation ship
several days ago.

00:15:00.047 --> 00:15:03.244
It did not appear
to be in any distress.

00:15:03.284 --> 00:15:04.842
Can you give us an idea
of its heading?

00:15:06.387 --> 00:15:09.151
It is possible
that information was recorded

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:11.158
in our sensor logs, Commander.

00:15:11.192 --> 00:15:15.356
Unfortunately, my entire crew
is occupied with our repairs!

00:15:15.396 --> 00:15:18.092
I don't think there will be time
to search our records.

00:15:20.134 --> 00:15:24.434
DaiMon, if we assigned
an engineering team

00:15:24.472 --> 00:15:26.872
to assist you
with your repair efforts

00:15:26.908 --> 00:15:29.775
would that give you time
to retrieve your logs?

00:15:29.811 --> 00:15:33.577
I believe it would, Captain.

00:15:37.485 --> 00:15:39.112
Spot?

00:15:39.153 --> 00:15:40.552
Spot.

00:15:40.588 --> 00:15:42.579
Spot... down.

00:15:43.591 --> 00:15:46.583
Spot... down.

00:15:47.595 --> 00:15:49.290
Down.

00:15:52.467 --> 00:15:55.027
Spot...

00:15:56.504 --> 00:15:57.664
down.

00:15:57.705 --> 00:15:59.138
This is down.

00:15:59.173 --> 00:16:01.505
Down is good.

00:16:01.542 --> 00:16:03.134
This is up.

00:16:03.177 --> 00:16:04.644
Up is no.

00:16:06.414 --> 00:16:08.507
One moment.
Come in.

00:16:08.549 --> 00:16:11.382
Data, I had
another idea that might help

00:16:11.419 --> 00:16:13.011
boost the power
conversion levels.

00:16:13.054 --> 00:16:14.021
Can you give me a hand?

00:16:14.055 --> 00:16:15.818
I would be happy to.

00:16:15.857 --> 00:16:17.791
Don't tell me you're
trying to teach the cat

00:16:17.825 --> 00:16:19.190
to use the console.

00:16:19.227 --> 00:16:20.717
It is part
of her training program.

00:16:20.761 --> 00:16:23.389
I am teaching Spot to jump down
from the desk on my command.

00:16:23.431 --> 00:16:25.228
How's it going?

00:16:26.267 --> 00:16:29.134
I have not been
entirely successful.

00:16:36.244 --> 00:16:40.180
Feline supplement number 221.

00:16:52.827 --> 00:16:53.851
Data

00:16:53.895 --> 00:16:57.353
that cat definitely
has a mind of her own.

00:16:57.398 --> 00:17:00.026
Maybe she's just not trainable.

00:17:00.067 --> 00:17:02.535
I suppose I must accept
that possibility.

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:05.733
It may be that Spot lacks
the intelligence necessary

00:17:05.773 --> 00:17:08.606
to learn the appropriate
responses to my commands.

00:17:09.877 --> 00:17:10.935
Hmm?

00:17:13.147 --> 00:17:15.513
Hmm.

00:17:18.986 --> 00:17:20.510
I don't know about Spot

00:17:20.555 --> 00:17:24.116
but, seems to me, your training
is coming along just fine.

00:17:24.158 --> 00:17:26.626
Come on, let's go.

00:17:32.266 --> 00:17:34.461
Data, keep the EPS
constant, will you

00:17:34.502 --> 00:17:36.868
while I remodulate
the power taps?

00:17:36.938 --> 00:17:38.735
This procedure
will only increase

00:17:38.773 --> 00:17:40.400
the conversion level by 0.8%.

00:17:40.441 --> 00:17:43.205
I know, but it could
be just enough.

00:17:43.244 --> 00:17:45.235
Enough?

00:17:45.279 --> 00:17:49.545
I just received this from
Commander Kaplan, subspace.

00:17:49.584 --> 00:17:52.576
"La Forge, I got

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:56.989
"the Intrepid's power
conversion levels up to 97.1%.

00:17:57.024 --> 00:17:58.685
"Maybe you should try cleaning

00:17:58.726 --> 00:18:00.694
your plasma grid
once in a while."

00:18:00.728 --> 00:18:02.389
Can you believe
the nerve of that guy?

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.261
We perform maintenance
on the plasma grid

00:18:04.298 --> 00:18:05.265
at regular intervals.

00:18:05.299 --> 00:18:06.698
I know.

00:18:06.734 --> 00:18:08.759
He's just trying
to get me angry.

00:18:10.471 --> 00:18:12.268
There.

00:18:12.306 --> 00:18:13.603
That should do it.

00:18:13.641 --> 00:18:17.077
Computer, what are the current
power conversion levels?

00:18:17.111 --> 00:18:22.276
Power conversion levels
are at 97.2%.

00:18:22.316 --> 00:18:24.580
Too bad, Mr. Kaplan.

00:18:26.487 --> 00:18:28.148
Captain's Log, supplemental.

00:18:28.189 --> 00:18:31.056
We have traced the Fleming's
most likely course.

00:18:31.092 --> 00:18:32.855
It now appears that the vessel

00:18:32.927 --> 00:18:35.088
may have come
to an unfortunate end.

00:18:35.129 --> 00:18:37.427
What's the origin
of this debris field?

00:18:37.465 --> 00:18:38.557
Unknown, sir.

00:18:38.599 --> 00:18:39.566
It does not appear

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.568
in any of the Federation charts
of the Corridor.

00:18:41.602 --> 00:18:44.070
Could it be what's left
of the Fleming?

00:18:44.105 --> 00:18:45.402
It is a possibility.

00:18:45.439 --> 00:18:47.236
The debris consists primarily

00:18:47.274 --> 00:18:49.435
of duranium
and polycomposite fragments

00:18:49.477 --> 00:18:51.138
which suggest a ship.

00:18:51.178 --> 00:18:53.874
Furthermore, the field
contains sufficient mass

00:18:53.914 --> 00:18:55.882
to account for the Fleming.

00:18:55.983 --> 00:18:57.041
All right.

00:18:57.084 --> 00:18:58.642
Let's take a closer look.

00:18:58.686 --> 00:19:00.881
Maybe we can find
something that will give us

00:19:00.921 --> 00:19:02.388
a more positive identification.

00:19:02.423 --> 00:19:04.948
Helm, take us through
the debris field.

00:19:04.992 --> 00:19:06.254
Ahead slow.

00:19:06.293 --> 00:19:09.387
Captain, I am picking up
a small, metallic object

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:12.627
approximately 3.5 meters
in diameter.

00:19:12.667 --> 00:19:15.830
Could be a ship's log recorder,
or a probe casing.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:17.303
I do not believe so.

00:19:17.338 --> 00:19:19.636
It is emitting
an unusual signal.

00:19:19.674 --> 00:19:21.665
Captain, it is
beginning to generate

00:19:21.709 --> 00:19:23.540
a verteron field.

00:19:23.577 --> 00:19:24.737
Shields up!

00:19:24.779 --> 00:19:26.246
Full reverse! Now!

00:19:30.584 --> 00:19:32.449
Damage report.

00:19:32.486 --> 00:19:34.750
Warp engines are off-line.

00:19:34.789 --> 00:19:37.019
Shields are down.

00:19:37.058 --> 00:19:39.652
All subspace systems
are inoperative.

00:19:39.694 --> 00:19:42.356
Just like the Ferengi vessel.

00:19:42.396 --> 00:19:44.057
Unidentified ship

00:19:44.098 --> 00:19:46.623
heading toward us...
two life-forms are aboard.

00:19:46.667 --> 00:19:48.191
On screen.

00:19:51.672 --> 00:19:53.503
Captain, they are initiating
their transporter system.

00:19:53.541 --> 00:19:55.441
Can we get more power
to the shields?

00:19:55.476 --> 00:19:57.273
No, sir.

00:19:57.311 --> 00:19:59.245
All decks, security alert!

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:00.770
Captain, we are being boarded.

00:20:01.982 --> 00:20:03.449
La Forge to Bridge.

00:20:03.484 --> 00:20:05.884
We have intruders
in Main Engineering.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:08.821
I need a security team
down here immediately.

00:20:08.856 --> 00:20:10.414
It's on its way.

00:20:10.458 --> 00:20:12.289
What do you want?

00:20:12.326 --> 00:20:14.351
We're trying to make you listen.

00:20:14.395 --> 00:20:16.329
You're killing us.

00:20:25.305 --> 00:20:27.500
You have made
a very serious accusation.

00:20:27.541 --> 00:20:28.906
I want you to explain it.

00:20:28.975 --> 00:20:31.876
Captain, according to our
research, warp fields cause

00:20:31.912 --> 00:20:34.403
a dangerous reaction
in this region of space.

00:20:34.448 --> 00:20:37.178
Our planet is
already being affected.

00:20:37.217 --> 00:20:39.742
We have measured large
gravitational shifts

00:20:39.786 --> 00:20:41.083
throughout our system.

00:20:41.121 --> 00:20:42.486
If something isn't done

00:20:42.522 --> 00:20:44.717
our planet will
become uninhabitable.

00:20:44.758 --> 00:20:46.248
Captain, I've heard
this theory before.

00:20:46.293 --> 00:20:49.353
Their research was evaluated by
the Federation Science Council

00:20:49.396 --> 00:20:50.385
a few years ago.

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.057
Quite frankly,
it didn't hold up.

00:20:52.098 --> 00:20:54.293
That research
was only preliminary.

00:20:54.334 --> 00:20:56.666
Our original analysis
was incomplete.

00:20:56.703 --> 00:20:58.568
If you wanted us
to review your research

00:20:58.605 --> 00:21:01.403
you should have made a request
through the Science Council.

00:21:01.441 --> 00:21:02.874
Their resources are limited.

00:21:02.976 --> 00:21:04.671
It would have taken over a year

00:21:04.711 --> 00:21:06.679
before they dispatched
a science ship

00:21:06.713 --> 00:21:08.374
to come and evaluate our work.

00:21:08.415 --> 00:21:10.383
We were not willing to wait
any longer.

00:21:10.417 --> 00:21:12.408
We knew that
if we disabled enough ships

00:21:12.452 --> 00:21:13.510
Starfleet would come.

00:21:13.553 --> 00:21:16.283
Then, at least, we would be able
to present our case.

00:21:16.323 --> 00:21:19.087
That's how you rationalize
these attacks?

00:21:19.125 --> 00:21:20.649
Neither you nor the Ferengi

00:21:20.694 --> 00:21:22.628
suffered any casualties,
Commander.

00:21:22.662 --> 00:21:25.426
The actual damage
to your vessels was negligible.

00:21:25.465 --> 00:21:28.025
We dispersed verteron probes
in the Corridor

00:21:28.068 --> 00:21:31.094
merely to disable warp-driven
ships... nothing more.

00:21:31.137 --> 00:21:33.697
You can call it whatever you
like, the fact remains

00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:35.401
you deliberately disguised
your probes.

00:21:35.442 --> 00:21:37.137
You made them
look like signal markers.

00:21:37.177 --> 00:21:38.610
You hid them
in the debris field.

00:21:38.645 --> 00:21:41.136
You mined the Corridor!

00:21:42.616 --> 00:21:44.277
Mr. La Forge,
how long do you estimate

00:21:44.317 --> 00:21:45.909
it will take
to restore the engines?

00:21:45.986 --> 00:21:48.921
Their verteron pulse overloaded
our field coils, Captain.

00:21:49.022 --> 00:21:50.922
It'll take at least 36 hours
to get underway.

00:21:50.957 --> 00:21:54.620
I know precisely how our pulse
affected your field coils.

00:21:54.661 --> 00:21:58.392
With my help, your ship could be
operational in ten hours...

00:21:58.431 --> 00:22:01.059
provided you agree
to review our research.

00:22:01.101 --> 00:22:03.569
Because of the seriousness
of your claim

00:22:03.603 --> 00:22:05.833
I am willing
to listen to your case.

00:22:05.872 --> 00:22:07.305
But let there be no mistake...

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:09.774
our priority here is
the recovery of the Fleming.

00:22:09.809 --> 00:22:12.300
Now, I expect you to help us
restore our engines

00:22:12.345 --> 00:22:14.540
and deactivate all
of the remaining probes

00:22:14.581 --> 00:22:15.639
in the Corridor.

00:22:15.682 --> 00:22:18.378
If you do not, you will both
be taken to the brig

00:22:18.418 --> 00:22:20.511
and from there
to the nearest Starbase

00:22:20.554 --> 00:22:23.114
where you will answer charges
for what you have done.

00:22:23.156 --> 00:22:26.250
My sister and I do not wish
to impede your rescue process.

00:22:26.293 --> 00:22:27.260
Rabal, don't.

00:22:27.294 --> 00:22:29.194
The probes are the only leverage
we have.

00:22:29.229 --> 00:22:31.527
Serova.

00:22:31.565 --> 00:22:37.435
We will do as you ask, Captain,
but please, reexamine our data.

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:38.596
We will.

00:22:38.638 --> 00:22:40.367
It is part of our job
to retain an open mind

00:22:40.407 --> 00:22:41.635
even under these circumstances.

00:22:41.675 --> 00:22:43.233
Mr. La Forge, take them
to Engineering.

00:22:43.276 --> 00:22:45.107
Get the core back on line.

00:22:45.145 --> 00:22:46.442
Yes, sir.

00:22:46.479 --> 00:22:48.674
Mr. Data, I want you
to review their research.

00:22:48.715 --> 00:22:51.149
I'd like your report
as soon as possible.

00:23:04.264 --> 00:23:06.630
There.
Shields have been restored.

00:23:06.666 --> 00:23:09.430
You will be able
to reengage your engines

00:23:09.469 --> 00:23:11.403
in approximately eight hours.

00:23:11.438 --> 00:23:14.134
You know, even when we get
the engines on line

00:23:14.174 --> 00:23:16.142
it's going to take
at least a week

00:23:16.176 --> 00:23:17.871
to recalibrate them properly.

00:23:17.944 --> 00:23:19.775
I'm sorry you've been
inconvenienced

00:23:19.813 --> 00:23:21.872
but that's all it is...
an inconvenience.

00:23:21.915 --> 00:23:23.746
Our concerns are
much more important

00:23:23.783 --> 00:23:25.580
than the condition
of your engines.

00:23:25.619 --> 00:23:27.780
What if the Fleming
had been transporting

00:23:27.821 --> 00:23:30.187
perishable supplies
or was on an emergency mission?

00:23:30.223 --> 00:23:32.453
Your little plan might
have cost a lot of lives.

00:23:32.492 --> 00:23:33.891
That didn't happen, Commander.

00:23:33.927 --> 00:23:37.624
And saving lives is precisely
what we're trying to do.

00:23:37.664 --> 00:23:38.631
Yeah, well, you have a very

00:23:38.665 --> 00:23:39.825
interesting way
of going about it.

00:23:39.866 --> 00:23:42.562
There's no point
in trying to talk to you.

00:23:42.602 --> 00:23:45.036
You've already decided
not to listen.

00:23:47.507 --> 00:23:52.206
Commander, I know my sister
can be somewhat, uh...

00:23:52.245 --> 00:23:53.337
aggravating.

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:54.745
I'd have to agree with that.

00:23:54.781 --> 00:23:56.373
But, please, try to understand...

00:23:56.416 --> 00:23:58.850
she believes profoundly
in this cause.

00:23:58.885 --> 00:24:00.546
She has sworn to dedicate
her life

00:24:00.587 --> 00:24:02.817
to exposing the dangers
of warp drive.

00:24:02.856 --> 00:24:05.620
Warp drive has been around
for three centuries.

00:24:05.659 --> 00:24:07.593
It's a proven technology.

00:24:07.627 --> 00:24:10.528
And you sound just like I did...
about four years ago.

00:24:10.563 --> 00:24:12.588
You mean, you didn't
believe her either?

00:24:12.632 --> 00:24:17.160
No. What I didn't realize was
just how brilliant my sister is.

00:24:17.203 --> 00:24:19.103
It took two years
of study for me

00:24:19.139 --> 00:24:22.302
to even grasp the principles
behind her theoretical models.

00:24:22.342 --> 00:24:24.503
They're that sophisticated.

00:24:24.544 --> 00:24:27.308
Well, our Commander Data
is no slouch.

00:24:27.347 --> 00:24:30.475
If there's anything there,
he'll find it.

00:24:30.517 --> 00:24:31.814
Well, I hope so.

00:24:31.851 --> 00:24:33.478
Because once we persuade
Starfleet

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:35.454
to stop warp travel
through the Corridor

00:24:35.488 --> 00:24:37.752
we're going to have
to convince our own people

00:24:37.791 --> 00:24:39.383
to give up warp drive
completely.

00:24:39.426 --> 00:24:42.793
Hekaras II is the only
inhabited world in this region.

00:24:42.829 --> 00:24:44.990
Without warp drive,
you'd be completely isolated

00:24:45.031 --> 00:24:47.693
from the rest of the Federation.

00:24:47.734 --> 00:24:50.897
Are you really willing
to take that step?

00:24:50.937 --> 00:24:53.201
Yes, and if you were
in my position

00:24:53.239 --> 00:24:56.367
I hope you'd be willing
to do the same thing.

00:25:05.251 --> 00:25:08.118
Serova and Rabal believe
that there are regions

00:25:08.154 --> 00:25:10.247
of potential subspace
instability within the Corridor.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:11.985
They believe
that if these regions

00:25:12.025 --> 00:25:14.721
continue to be exposed
to warp field energy

00:25:14.761 --> 00:25:16.194
they will rupture.

00:25:16.229 --> 00:25:19.096
Subspace will extrude into
normal space, forming a rift.

00:25:19.132 --> 00:25:21.532
It's like pacing up and down
on the same piece of carpet...

00:25:21.568 --> 00:25:22.728
eventually you wear it out.

00:25:22.769 --> 00:25:25.260
That analogy
is essentially correct, sir.

00:25:25.305 --> 00:25:26.966
Can this theory be proven?

00:25:27.006 --> 00:25:27.995
Not at this time, sir.

00:25:28.041 --> 00:25:30.339
There is insufficient
evidence to do so.

00:25:30.376 --> 00:25:33.072
In order to determine
whether or not a rift will form

00:25:33.113 --> 00:25:35.581
a region must be exposed
to warp field energy

00:25:35.615 --> 00:25:37.776
approximately
one million times greater

00:25:37.817 --> 00:25:40.217
than that normally generated
by a starship.

00:25:40.253 --> 00:25:42.881
Commander, we believe the warp
field effect is cumulative.

00:25:42.956 --> 00:25:45.481
Each starship that passes
through the Corridor at warp

00:25:45.525 --> 00:25:48.426
brings us one step closer
to forming a rift.

00:25:48.461 --> 00:25:50.258
I agree that is a possibility.

00:25:51.498 --> 00:25:54.763
Captain, I suggest we ask
the Federation Science Council

00:25:54.801 --> 00:25:57.065
to send a research vessel
to this area.

00:25:57.103 --> 00:25:58.468
A more detailed investigation

00:25:58.505 --> 00:26:00.234
would resolve
many of our questions.

00:26:00.273 --> 00:26:04.505
That's your response...
more research, more delays.

00:26:04.544 --> 00:26:07.536
I suppose I shouldn't have
expected anything different.

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:09.047
Bridge to Captain Picard.

00:26:09.082 --> 00:26:11.016
Go ahead, Number One.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:13.610
We've located the Fleming
on long-range sensors...

00:26:13.653 --> 00:26:15.848
approximately
0.3 light years away.

00:26:15.955 --> 00:26:17.320
What's her condition?

00:26:17.357 --> 00:26:18.984
The vessel appears to be intact.

00:26:19.025 --> 00:26:20.549
They have shields, but it looks

00:26:20.593 --> 00:26:22.424
like their subspace systems
are out.

00:26:22.462 --> 00:26:23.793
Very well.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:26.128
We'll be under way as soon
as our engines are on line.

00:26:26.166 --> 00:26:27.133
Aye, sir.

00:26:27.167 --> 00:26:28.862
I would like you all
to put together

00:26:28.968 --> 00:26:31.198
a research proposal
for the Science Council.

00:26:31.237 --> 00:26:33.137
I will give it
my full recommendation.

00:26:33.173 --> 00:26:35.300
Captain, I appreciate the
opportunity you're giving us.

00:26:35.341 --> 00:26:37.241
It's a beginning that we...

00:26:37.277 --> 00:26:38.335
Not "we."

00:26:38.378 --> 00:26:40.812
You can do whatever you want,
Rabal.

00:26:40.847 --> 00:26:42.872
I'll have no part of this.

00:26:42.949 --> 00:26:44.917
It's just another delay.

00:26:49.923 --> 00:26:52.289
Captain, the engines
are back on line.

00:26:52.325 --> 00:26:53.724
All systems are ready.

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:54.784
Very good.

00:26:54.828 --> 00:26:56.261
Helm, lay in a course
for the Fleming.

00:26:56.296 --> 00:26:57.490
Aye, sir.

00:26:57.530 --> 00:26:59.293
They appear to be
in pretty good condition.

00:26:59.332 --> 00:27:01.766
Our sensors show their shields
are back to full strength.

00:27:01.801 --> 00:27:05.362
Captain, the Hekaran ship
is moving off.

00:27:07.607 --> 00:27:10.269
There is one person on board.

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:12.471
It is Serova.

00:27:12.512 --> 00:27:13.809
Doctor, what is going on?

00:27:14.848 --> 00:27:16.145
I don't know.

00:27:16.182 --> 00:27:19.083
Captain, may I speak to her?

00:27:19.118 --> 00:27:20.676
Mr. Worf, open a channel.

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:24.156
Serova, what are you
trying to do?

00:27:24.190 --> 00:27:26.021
They wanted proof.

00:27:26.059 --> 00:27:28.755
I'm going to give it to them.

00:27:28.795 --> 00:27:30.854
I'm sorry.

00:27:34.234 --> 00:27:36.634
Captain,
the Hekaran ship's engines

00:27:36.669 --> 00:27:38.796
are beginning to overload.

00:27:38.838 --> 00:27:41.306
I believe Serova
is attempting to create

00:27:41.341 --> 00:27:43.104
a warp core breach.

00:27:43.142 --> 00:27:45.167
No.

00:27:45.211 --> 00:27:47.543
Prepare for impact.

00:27:50.216 --> 00:27:52.616
Shields at maximum.
Full reverse.

00:27:54.587 --> 00:27:55.884
Report.

00:27:55.922 --> 00:27:57.412
We are clear of the shock wave.

00:27:57.457 --> 00:28:01.689
However, tetryon flux in
the area is increasing rapidly.

00:28:14.040 --> 00:28:16.235
It's incredible.

00:28:16.275 --> 00:28:19.472
Mr. Data,
what's your analysis?

00:28:19.512 --> 00:28:22.948
The event is approximately
0.1 light years in diameter

00:28:22.982 --> 00:28:25.849
and it is emitting
extremely high levels

00:28:25.951 --> 00:28:27.475
of tetryon radiation.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:30.819
It appears to be what Rabal
and Serova predicted...

00:28:30.856 --> 00:28:31.845
a subspace rift.

00:28:31.924 --> 00:28:33.789
She was right.

00:28:33.826 --> 00:28:35.293
Can you get a fix
on the Fleming?

00:28:35.327 --> 00:28:36.521
Is she still in one piece?

00:28:36.562 --> 00:28:37.859
Scanning.

00:28:37.930 --> 00:28:40.763
The Fleming is near
its previous coordinates.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:44.292
It appears to be intact,
but it is now within the rift.

00:28:44.336 --> 00:28:48.033
Doctor, is it possible
for a ship to survive in there?

00:28:50.376 --> 00:28:53.402
Doctor, I'm sorry about your
loss, but we need your help now.

00:28:55.181 --> 00:28:56.580
Of course, Captain.

00:28:56.615 --> 00:28:59.607
Does the rift pose any danger
to the Fleming?

00:28:59.652 --> 00:29:01.176
I don't know.

00:29:04.390 --> 00:29:06.358
What was that?

00:29:06.392 --> 00:29:08.257
Some kind of high-energy
distortion wave

00:29:08.294 --> 00:29:10.956
Looks like they're being
generated from within the rift.

00:29:10.996 --> 00:29:13.260
If we maintain
our current position

00:29:13.299 --> 00:29:15.460
our shields should provide
sufficient protection.

00:29:15.501 --> 00:29:16.468
What about the Fleming?

00:29:16.502 --> 00:29:17.969
How long can they survive
in there?

00:29:18.003 --> 00:29:21.166
The waves are even more intense
within the rift.

00:29:21.207 --> 00:29:23.698
I estimate her shields will fail

00:29:23.742 --> 00:29:24.766
in approximately 12 hours.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:26.607
Can we risk going in there
to get them out?

00:29:26.645 --> 00:29:28.044
Not at warp, Commander.

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:29.274
According to these readings

00:29:29.315 --> 00:29:31.545
the rift is in a state
of accelerating instability

00:29:31.584 --> 00:29:33.347
which would make it
extremely sensitive

00:29:33.385 --> 00:29:34.409
to warp field energies.

00:29:34.453 --> 00:29:35.420
If we go in at warp

00:29:35.454 --> 00:29:36.819
we might expand it even further.

00:29:36.856 --> 00:29:38.721
I don't think we have a choice.

00:29:38.757 --> 00:29:41.590
It would take weeks
to reach them at impulse.

00:29:41.627 --> 00:29:43.219
I want some better options.

00:29:43.262 --> 00:29:46.231
Data, Geordi, take Dr. Rabal
and begin an analysis

00:29:46.265 --> 00:29:47.459
of the rift.

00:29:49.368 --> 00:29:53.771
We need to find some way to get
the Fleming out of there.

00:29:53.806 --> 00:29:59.108
Second Officer's Log,
Stardate 47312.1.

00:29:59.145 --> 00:30:01.511
Our new sensor readings
have greatly improved

00:30:01.547 --> 00:30:03.139
our understanding of the rift.

00:30:03.182 --> 00:30:05.742
However, we have been
unable to find

00:30:05.784 --> 00:30:07.581
any way to counteract it.

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:10.088
Everything here supports
Serova's theories.

00:30:10.122 --> 00:30:11.612
I can't find any way

00:30:11.657 --> 00:30:14.319
to close the rift
or even reduce its size.

00:30:16.328 --> 00:30:17.886
Doctor, is it possible

00:30:17.997 --> 00:30:21.763
there are areas inside
the rift which are stable

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:25.167
where we can safely
use our warp engines?

00:30:25.204 --> 00:30:26.501
Perhaps.

00:30:26.539 --> 00:30:31.909
Computer, scan the rift
for regions of low instability.

00:30:31.977 --> 00:30:35.504
There's no area stable enough
to withstand a warp pulse.

00:30:35.548 --> 00:30:36.913
Wait a minute.

00:30:36.982 --> 00:30:38.916
This is strange.

00:30:38.951 --> 00:30:41.920
Computer, display grid delta-17.

00:30:43.289 --> 00:30:44.950
Enhance and magnify.

00:30:46.992 --> 00:30:49.654
What do you make of that?

00:30:49.695 --> 00:30:54.291
It's a subspace instability...
outside the rift.

00:30:54.333 --> 00:30:56.528
That should not be possible.

00:30:56.569 --> 00:30:58.093
Commander, I'm going to begin

00:30:58.137 --> 00:31:00.571
a field-enhanced scan
of this sector.

00:31:00.606 --> 00:31:03.473
I'd like to get
a closer look at this.

00:31:03.509 --> 00:31:05.067
In the meantime,
we'd better tell the Captain

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:06.543
we don't know how to get
to the Fleming.

00:31:06.579 --> 00:31:08.979
I am not certain that is true.

00:31:09.014 --> 00:31:11.073
I believe I have an idea.

00:31:15.754 --> 00:31:18.086
I suggest
we coast into the rift.

00:31:18.123 --> 00:31:19.420
Coast?

00:31:19.458 --> 00:31:22.359
We can initiate a brief,
high-intensity warp pulse

00:31:22.394 --> 00:31:24.089
from our current position.

00:31:24.129 --> 00:31:25.892
We should be able to attain

00:31:25.931 --> 00:31:27.831
sufficient velocity
to enter the rift

00:31:27.866 --> 00:31:29.333
beam the crew off the Fleming

00:31:29.368 --> 00:31:32.462
and exit without using
our warp engines.

00:31:32.504 --> 00:31:35.473
All right, let's say we initiate
a full-power warp pulse.

00:31:35.507 --> 00:31:37.873
How much time do we need
to drop out of warp?

00:31:37.910 --> 00:31:39.878
If we field-saturate
the nacelles

00:31:39.912 --> 00:31:42.244
we should be able
to sustain warp speed

00:31:42.281 --> 00:31:44.146
for approximately two minutes.

00:31:44.183 --> 00:31:46.048
That doesn't give us much time.

00:31:46.085 --> 00:31:48.383
And it could get
pretty rough in there.

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:50.513
Our timing will be critical.

00:31:50.556 --> 00:31:52.148
Begin your calculations,
Mr. Data.

00:31:52.191 --> 00:31:53.920
We'll go as soon
as you're ready.

00:31:53.959 --> 00:31:55.358
Aye, sir.

00:32:00.833 --> 00:32:05.532
I believe the maximum saturation
level should be 18.3%.

00:32:05.571 --> 00:32:07.300
All right.

00:32:07.339 --> 00:32:08.328
With these parameters

00:32:08.374 --> 00:32:10.308
we should be able
to maintain warp speed

00:32:10.342 --> 00:32:11.969
for... two minutes,
eight seconds.

00:32:12.011 --> 00:32:14.036
I don't think we can get
any more than that.

00:32:14.079 --> 00:32:16.775
I agree, but based
on the size of the rift

00:32:16.815 --> 00:32:20.182
it should give us sufficient
time to complete the rescue.

00:32:20.219 --> 00:32:24.781
Computer, initiate
nacelle field saturation.

00:32:24.823 --> 00:32:27.223
How did we miss it, Data?

00:32:27.259 --> 00:32:29.557
I beg your pardon.

00:32:29.595 --> 00:32:31.563
How did we miss the connection

00:32:31.597 --> 00:32:34.896
between warp drive
and the formation of the rift?

00:32:34.933 --> 00:32:36.093
Between the two of us

00:32:36.135 --> 00:32:38.865
we've logged thousands of hours
on these engines.

00:32:38.904 --> 00:32:41.134
We're supposed
to be warp field experts.

00:32:41.173 --> 00:32:44.370
We certainly were wrong
this time.

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:46.571
Technically, Geordi,
we were not wrong.

00:32:46.612 --> 00:32:48.546
Serova's theories
rested on assumptions

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:50.639
which were unprovable.

00:32:50.683 --> 00:32:52.514
Seems to me, she managed
to prove them

00:32:52.551 --> 00:32:53.711
pretty conclusively.

00:32:53.752 --> 00:32:54.912
By using methods

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:58.387
any reputable scientist
would never employ.

00:32:58.424 --> 00:33:02.861
Yeah, she was willing to die
in order to make her point.

00:33:02.961 --> 00:33:07.057
We should have listened to her
more closely, Data.

00:33:09.134 --> 00:33:12.592
We reviewed Serova's research
to the best of our abilities

00:33:12.638 --> 00:33:15.835
and we were prepared to continue
studying the problem

00:33:15.874 --> 00:33:17.569
but that was unsatisfactory.

00:33:17.609 --> 00:33:19.873
Yeah, I can remember times
when I was a little stubborn.

00:33:19.945 --> 00:33:21.310
Trying to get people
to believe me

00:33:21.347 --> 00:33:22.644
when I didn't have enough proof.

00:33:22.681 --> 00:33:25.377
I do not believe you would have
resorted to such extremes.

00:33:25.417 --> 00:33:27.248
But she had to...

00:33:27.286 --> 00:33:30.221
just to get us to listen.

00:33:31.957 --> 00:33:34.551
Why was I so resistant?

00:33:34.593 --> 00:33:36.891
Perhaps because
her aggressive methods

00:33:36.929 --> 00:33:39.762
created
an adversarial situation.

00:33:39.798 --> 00:33:41.231
Yeah.

00:33:41.266 --> 00:33:47.796
Maybe I was taking
the whole thing personally.

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:50.331
I do not understand.

00:33:53.545 --> 00:33:57.379
Maybe... I was
a little threatened.

00:33:57.416 --> 00:33:59.043
The thought that warp engines

00:33:59.084 --> 00:34:01.052
might be doing
some kind of damage.

00:34:06.892 --> 00:34:09.156
It's going to take
another half an hour

00:34:09.194 --> 00:34:10.855
for the nacelles to saturate.

00:34:10.896 --> 00:34:12.955
Will you call me
when they're done?

00:34:39.258 --> 00:34:40.316
Commander.

00:34:40.359 --> 00:34:42.691
May I join you?

00:34:51.303 --> 00:34:54.431
I wanted to say I'm sorry
about your sister.

00:34:56.642 --> 00:34:58.132
Thank you.

00:34:59.711 --> 00:35:01.645
I'm trying to tell myself

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:04.740
that she died
for what she believed in

00:35:04.783 --> 00:35:07.684
but, somehow,
that isn't much comfort.

00:35:07.719 --> 00:35:10.210
I wish it could have happened
differently.

00:35:10.255 --> 00:35:14.021
Maybe I should have looked
at the research more closely.

00:35:14.059 --> 00:35:15.686
It's possible there's something
that I missed.

00:35:15.727 --> 00:35:18.560
No, you didn't miss anything.

00:35:18.597 --> 00:35:20.656
The problem was time.

00:35:20.699 --> 00:35:25.159
We needed more time
to do the proper research.

00:35:25.204 --> 00:35:28.173
Serova wasn't willing to wait.

00:35:28.207 --> 00:35:33.577
No. I guess the question is,
now, where do we go from here?

00:35:35.747 --> 00:35:38.272
I don't think we can look
at space travel

00:35:38.317 --> 00:35:39.614
the same way anymore.

00:35:39.651 --> 00:35:41.585
We're going to have to change.

00:35:41.620 --> 00:35:44.487
I've been in Starfleet
a long time.

00:35:44.523 --> 00:35:46.548
We depend on warp drive.

00:35:46.592 --> 00:35:51.052
I just don't know how easy
it's going to be to change.

00:35:53.765 --> 00:35:56.393
It won't be easy at all.

00:36:00.805 --> 00:36:03.365
Captain, warp pulse calculations
are complete.

00:36:03.408 --> 00:36:06.400
We will be firing the engines
at maximum intensity

00:36:06.444 --> 00:36:08.503
for 6.3 seconds
before disengaging.

00:36:08.546 --> 00:36:11.037
Damage control teams
are standing by on all decks.

00:36:11.082 --> 00:36:13.573
The course to the Fleming
is plotted and laid in, sir.

00:36:13.618 --> 00:36:17.315
Mr. La Forge,
whenever you're ready.

00:36:17.355 --> 00:36:19.482
Aye, sir.

00:36:19.524 --> 00:36:23.051
Initiating warp pulse... now.

00:36:28.332 --> 00:36:30.061
Engines disengaged.

00:36:30.101 --> 00:36:32.831
We are now entering the rift.

00:36:33.905 --> 00:36:35.998
Hull stress is increasing...

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:39.100
83% of maximum tolerance
and rising.

00:36:39.143 --> 00:36:40.838
How long until we reach
the Fleming?

00:36:40.878 --> 00:36:42.869
One minute, 22 seconds.

00:36:42.947 --> 00:36:45.347
All transporter rooms
are standing by.

00:36:45.383 --> 00:36:48.580
Captain, I'm picking up power
emissions from the Fleming.

00:36:48.619 --> 00:36:52.453
They may be attempting
to initiate their warp drives.

00:36:52.490 --> 00:36:54.219
They might have been able
to repair their engines.

00:36:54.258 --> 00:36:56.556
Captain, if they activate...

00:36:56.594 --> 00:36:57.583
I know, Mr. Data.

00:36:57.628 --> 00:36:59.493
Can we establish communication?

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:02.294
No, sir... subspace interference
is too high.

00:37:02.333 --> 00:37:04.665
How long until we reach
transporter range?

00:37:04.702 --> 00:37:05.669
53 seconds.

00:37:05.703 --> 00:37:08.934
I don't think
we're going to make it.

00:37:08.973 --> 00:37:11.703
Captain, they have initiated
their warp drive.

00:37:13.644 --> 00:37:14.668
Report.

00:37:14.712 --> 00:37:17.681
Damage on decks six and 14.

00:37:17.715 --> 00:37:19.842
Our structural integrity field
is holding.

00:37:19.884 --> 00:37:21.249
What about the Fleming?

00:37:21.285 --> 00:37:22.513
They are badly damaged, sir.

00:37:22.553 --> 00:37:24.316
Their life-support
systems are failing.

00:37:24.355 --> 00:37:25.652
All transporter rooms

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:28.181
prepare emergency
evacuation procedures.

00:37:28.226 --> 00:37:32.788
Captain, when the Fleming
activated their warp drive

00:37:32.830 --> 00:37:35.264
the rift increased by 2.3%.

00:37:35.299 --> 00:37:38.234
We no longer have
sufficient momentum to escape.

00:37:52.049 --> 00:37:56.247
Distortion wave intensity has
increased by a factor of ten.

00:37:56.287 --> 00:37:59.779
We are within transporter range
of the Fleming, sir.

00:37:59.824 --> 00:38:01.951
Commencing evacuation
procedures.

00:38:03.561 --> 00:38:06.962
Distortion waves occurring
every 59 seconds.

00:38:06.998 --> 00:38:10.229
Hull stress is nearing
maximum tolerance.

00:38:10.268 --> 00:38:11.826
Data, what if we forced
an EPS discharge

00:38:11.869 --> 00:38:12.961
through the impulse reactor?

00:38:13.004 --> 00:38:14.733
Would that be enough
to get us out of here?

00:38:14.772 --> 00:38:16.205
I do not believe so, sir

00:38:16.240 --> 00:38:19.073
and the resulting explosion
would likely destroy

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:21.169
the saucer section
in the process.

00:38:21.212 --> 00:38:22.975
Captain, we may be able
to get out of here

00:38:23.014 --> 00:38:24.345
without using the warp engines.

00:38:24.382 --> 00:38:25.474
How, Geordi?

00:38:25.516 --> 00:38:27.609
When was the last time
you went surfing, Commander?

00:38:28.853 --> 00:38:30.377
A distortion wave?

00:38:30.421 --> 00:38:31.513
Exactly, Captain.

00:38:31.556 --> 00:38:34.184
If we can phase-match
our deflector shield

00:38:34.225 --> 00:38:36.159
to the EM variants
of the distortion wave

00:38:36.194 --> 00:38:37.218
when the next one hits

00:38:37.261 --> 00:38:38.694
we'll be pulled along with it.

00:38:38.729 --> 00:38:40.788
We could ride it
through the rift

00:38:40.832 --> 00:38:43.266
and then break away
once we're clear...

00:38:43.301 --> 00:38:45.269
that is, if we survive the ride.

00:38:45.303 --> 00:38:46.793
Is everyone off the Fleming?

00:38:46.838 --> 00:38:49.306
The last of the crew
has beamed aboard, sir.

00:38:49.340 --> 00:38:51.467
All right, Mr. La Forge.

00:38:51.509 --> 00:38:53.170
Get us out of here.

00:38:53.211 --> 00:38:54.371
Yes, sir.

00:38:54.412 --> 00:38:57.245
Phase matching
the deflector shields now.

00:38:57.281 --> 00:39:00.478
The next distortion wave
will impact in 14 seconds.

00:39:00.518 --> 00:39:02.145
Matching phases...

00:39:02.186 --> 00:39:07.180
Prepare to engage
the deflector shield on my mark.

00:39:07.225 --> 00:39:08.283
Ten seconds.

00:39:09.493 --> 00:39:12.553
And... mark!

00:39:12.597 --> 00:39:15.361
Engage deflector shield,
full power.

00:39:20.271 --> 00:39:21.568
We lost it.

00:39:21.606 --> 00:39:23.335
We were unable to maintain
phase match

00:39:23.374 --> 00:39:24.807
with the distortion wave, sir.

00:39:24.842 --> 00:39:26.104
What went wrong, Mr. La Forge?

00:39:26.143 --> 00:39:28.611
The speed differential
was too high, Captain.

00:39:28.646 --> 00:39:30.739
Another distortion wave
approaching.

00:39:30.781 --> 00:39:33.079
Impact in 26 seconds.

00:39:33.117 --> 00:39:34.846
Our hull stress is
already critical.

00:39:34.919 --> 00:39:37.114
We can't handle
many more jolts like that.

00:39:37.154 --> 00:39:39.850
Captain, I suggest we take
the ship to full impulse.

00:39:39.924 --> 00:39:41.619
If we can attain
sufficient speed

00:39:41.659 --> 00:39:44.059
it will lessen the impact
when the wave hits.

00:39:44.095 --> 00:39:45.062
Helm, full impulse.

00:39:45.096 --> 00:39:46.085
Head us out of the rift.

00:39:46.130 --> 00:39:47.791
Mr. Worf, channel
all available power

00:39:47.832 --> 00:39:49.424
to the structural
integrity fields.

00:39:49.467 --> 00:39:50.434
Aye, sir.

00:39:50.468 --> 00:39:51.867
Stand by, deflector shields.

00:39:51.903 --> 00:39:52.870
We're ready, Captain.

00:39:52.970 --> 00:39:55.438
Distortion wave impact
in five seconds.

00:39:55.473 --> 00:39:58.465
Engage deflector shield... now!

00:40:02.513 --> 00:40:04.845
We are now
within the distortion wave.

00:40:04.882 --> 00:40:08.147
Hull stresses have
exceeded maximum tolerance.

00:40:08.185 --> 00:40:11.177
Structural integrity fields
are failing on decks ten

00:40:11.222 --> 00:40:12.189
through 16.

00:40:12.223 --> 00:40:13.485
Switching to backups.

00:40:13.524 --> 00:40:15.992
We're separating
from the distortion wave.

00:40:16.027 --> 00:40:17.790
I'm attempting to compensate.

00:40:17.828 --> 00:40:19.420
How much longer
until we clear the rift?

00:40:19.463 --> 00:40:21.727
Approximately 12 seconds, sir.

00:40:21.766 --> 00:40:24.530
Hull stress
is 120% above tolerance.

00:40:24.568 --> 00:40:26.593
A structural breach is imminent.

00:40:35.279 --> 00:40:37.474
We have cleared the rift, sir.

00:40:37.515 --> 00:40:40.279
Hull stress has
returned to normal.

00:40:41.285 --> 00:40:43.553
Cancel Red Alert.

00:40:43.686 --> 00:40:47.588
Captain's Log, Stardate 47314.5.

00:40:47.623 --> 00:40:49.648
We have been continuing
our research

00:40:49.692 --> 00:40:51.421
while the Federation Council

00:40:51.460 --> 00:40:54.793
studies our preliminary reports
on the subspace rift.

00:40:54.830 --> 00:40:57.628
It now appears certain
that what we've seen here

00:40:57.667 --> 00:41:01.535
will have repercussions
for many years to come.

00:41:01.571 --> 00:41:03.869
Our scans show a lot
of subspace instabilities

00:41:03.906 --> 00:41:05.032
throughout the sector.

00:41:05.074 --> 00:41:07.201
If they're exposed
to enough warp energies

00:41:07.243 --> 00:41:09.575
they could eventually
create other rifts.

00:41:09.612 --> 00:41:10.772
Dr. Rabal?

00:41:10.813 --> 00:41:12.678
Based on current
warp drive patterns

00:41:12.715 --> 00:41:15.980
in this sector, we've projected
where subspace rifts

00:41:16.018 --> 00:41:19.545
will be most likely to be formed
over the next 40 years.

00:41:19.589 --> 00:41:22.353
This is how things are now.

00:41:22.391 --> 00:41:26.555
This is how they'll look
in ten years...

00:41:26.596 --> 00:41:28.894
20 years...

00:41:30.800 --> 00:41:32.927
30 years...

00:41:34.537 --> 00:41:36.835
40 years.

00:41:38.274 --> 00:41:40.003
Thank you, Doctor.

00:41:40.042 --> 00:41:41.407
Mr. La Forge.

00:41:43.846 --> 00:41:46.371
Ah...

00:41:46.415 --> 00:41:48.144
We've received new directives

00:41:48.184 --> 00:41:50.744
from the Federation Council
on this matter.

00:41:50.786 --> 00:41:52.048
Until we can find a way

00:41:52.088 --> 00:41:53.919
to counteract
the warp field effect

00:41:54.023 --> 00:41:55.547
the Council feels
our best course

00:41:55.591 --> 00:41:57.616
is to slow the damage
as much as possible.

00:41:57.660 --> 00:42:00.629
Therefore, areas of space
found susceptible to warp fields

00:42:00.663 --> 00:42:02.494
will be restricted
to essential travel only

00:42:02.531 --> 00:42:05.125
and, effective immediately,
all Federation vessels

00:42:05.167 --> 00:42:06.725
will be limited to a speed...

00:42:08.237 --> 00:42:10.000
of warp five

00:42:10.039 --> 00:42:13.873
except in cases
of extreme emergency.

00:42:13.943 --> 00:42:15.877
Well, the Klingons
will observe these restrictions

00:42:15.978 --> 00:42:17.036
but the Romulans will not.

00:42:17.079 --> 00:42:18.637
And what about the Ferengi

00:42:18.681 --> 00:42:20.808
and the Cardassians,
for that matter?

00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:22.977
The Federation
is sharing all our data

00:42:23.019 --> 00:42:24.077
with warp-capable species.

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:26.645
We can only hope
that they realize

00:42:26.689 --> 00:42:29.157
it's in their own interests
and take similar action.

00:42:29.191 --> 00:42:30.522
Putting limits on warp speed

00:42:30.559 --> 00:42:32.891
is only going to prevent
other rifts from forming.

00:42:32.962 --> 00:42:35.055
What are we going
to do about this one?

00:42:35.097 --> 00:42:37.156
Unfortunately, right now,
there's not much we can do.

00:42:37.199 --> 00:42:38.393
The gravitational shifts

00:42:38.434 --> 00:42:40.732
have already begun
to affect my planet's orbit.

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:43.330
Our climate is changing.

00:42:43.372 --> 00:42:44.498
The Federation is setting up

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:46.064
a weather control matrix
on Hekaras II.

00:42:46.108 --> 00:42:48.633
It's only a temporary solution,
but it should suffice for now.

00:42:48.678 --> 00:42:50.202
We're lucky the rift took place

00:42:50.246 --> 00:42:52.077
as far away
from the planet as it did.

00:42:52.114 --> 00:42:54.378
It gives us some time
to consider our options.

00:42:54.417 --> 00:42:56.647
If there are no more questions,
you're all dismissed.

00:43:11.067 --> 00:43:13.331
We should have
those thermal stabilizers ready

00:43:13.369 --> 00:43:15.098
for the Hekarans in another day,
sir.

00:43:15.137 --> 00:43:16.161
Very well.

00:43:16.205 --> 00:43:17.866
You know, Geordi

00:43:17.973 --> 00:43:21.636
I've spent the better part
of my life exploring space.

00:43:21.677 --> 00:43:27.707
I've charted new worlds,
I've met dozens of new species

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:32.084
and I believe that these were
all valuable ends in themselves.

00:43:32.121 --> 00:43:36.057
And now it seems that...
all this while

00:43:36.092 --> 00:43:42.053
I was helping to damage
the thing that I hold most dear.

00:43:42.098 --> 00:43:44.896
It won't turn out that way,
Captain.

00:43:44.967 --> 00:43:47.697
We still have time
to make it better.
